- **Tuning confound:** V1 uses fixed defaults; V2 shows sensitivity but the two use different selection procedures, so V2 is a robustness check, not a fairer headline.", "evidence": "Folds researcher's 5 requests: (1) N=5000 added as 7th ladder point; (2) GP N=2000 one-line wall-clock: O(N^3)~8e9 ops/fit x3 seeds x1 config ~2.4e10 ops, seconds-to-minutes on 224 cores, no GPU; (3) V2 inner-CV grids explicit and each <=20 configs, stated as fixed grid not Optuna; (4) Table 1 (V1 fixed-defaults) is the headline primary result, Table 3 (V2 tuned) is a sensitivity check; (5) feasibility section plainly states a pre-provisioned env (numpy/pandas/scikit-learn/scikit-optimize) is required and current /workspace has none with network disabled. Structure follows requested 8 sections. Prior verified facts carried: /workspace has only task.json, no dataset, Python 3.14.4, no numpy/sklearn, 224 cores, no GPU.", "assumptions": ["Dataset not physically present; 26-param schema (23 continuous + 3 categorical: body style/front bumper/rear bumper), total N ~10k, per-class counts, and scalar Cd target all assumed from published DrivAerNet++ (Saez et al.), flagged unverified", "Body style treated as both input feature (one-hot) and stratification axis", "GP excluded above N=2000 (or sparse variant) due to O(N^3)", "Execution requires a pre-provisioned offline Python environment (numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, scikit-optimize/GPyTorch); not currently available in /workspace"], "uncertainties": ["Exact 26 parameter names and continuous/categorical split unconfirmed offline", "Per-body-style sample counts and exact total N unknown", "Whether target should be scalar Cd only or the full coefficient vector", "Whether 3 seeds suffice to declare an architecture crossover conclusive", "GP high-N behavior (N=5000, full) not directly measured due to cubic cost"], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-76eaacca-1819-4749-9e6c-1b974b968ea5", "tool_call": {"id": "call-76eaacca-1819-4749-9e6c-1b974b968ea5", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"uncertainties": ["Exact 26 parameter names and continuous/categorical split unconfirmed offline", "Per-body-style sample counts and exact total N unknown", "Whether target should be scalar Cd only or the full coefficient vector", "Whether 3 seeds suffice to declare an architecture crossover conclusive", "GP high-N behavior (N=5000, full) not directly measured due to cubic cost"], "assumptions": ["Dataset not physically present; 26-param schema (23 continuous + 3 categorical: body style/front bumper/rear bumper), total N ~10k, per-class counts, and scalar Cd target all assumed from published DrivAerNet++ (Saez et al.), flagged unverified", "Body style treated as both input feature (one-hot) and stratification axis", "GP excluded above N=2000 (or sparse variant) due to O(N^3)", "Execution requires a pre-provisioned offline Python environment (numpy, pandas, scikit-learn, scikit-optimize/GPyTorch); not currently available in /workspace"], "answer": "# Experiment Design \u2014 Surrogate-model scalability & architecture comparison for aerodynamic drag on DrivAerNet++\n\n**Question under test:** Does predictive performance of surrogate models (a) improve with increased training-set size, and (b) differ across architectures, for drag prediction on 26-parameter car designs (fastback / notchback / estateback)?\n\n---\n\n## 1. Rationale\nThe question decomposes into two hypotheses:\n- **H1 (scalability):** for a fixed architecture, test error decreases monotonically as training N grows (learning-curve effect).\n- **H2 (architecture):** at a given N, architectures rank differently; and \u2014 the interesting case \u2014 the ranking *reverses* with N (kernel methods lead at small N, deep nets lead at large N).\n\nDesign logic: fix a single held-out test set and a nested stratified training-subset ladder so the *only* thing that changes between points is N (no leakage, no test-set drift). Compare 5 architectures spanning the small-data\u2192large-data spectrum (polynomial floor \u2192 GP \u2192 tree ensembles \u2192 MLP) so a performance crossover, if any, is visible. Report per-body-style to catch whether one style drives the effect.\n\n## 2. Data & Assumptions\n**Repository state (verified):** `/workspace` contains only `task.json`. No DrivAerNet++ CSV/HDF5/JSON/README/paper/split/normalisation files are present. All data specifics below are **assumed from the published DrivAerNet++ paper (Saez et al.) and flagged as unverified** \u2014 the executing team must confirm against the actual dataset file.\n\n- **Sample size:** assume **~10k total** (range 8k\u201313k); treat \"full\" as the actual available N. Per-class counts unknown \u2014 assume roughly balanced, verify.\n- **26 design parameters:** assume **23 continuous + 3 categorical**. Categoricals: **body style** (fastback/notchback/estateback), **front bumper style**, **rear bumper style**. Continuous: geometric (overhangs, wheelbase, track, height, width, panel angles, wheel diameters/offsets, etc.). *Exact 26 names/split to be confirmed from dataset metadata.*\n- **Target:** single scalar **drag coefficient Cd**. (Dataset stores a coefficient vector; we fit/regress only Cd. If multi-output is desired this design must be re-scoped.)\n- **Encoding:** one-hot the 3 categoricals (body style \u2192 3 dummies, bumpers \u2192 their level counts). Continuous standardized (zero mean / unit var) using **training-subset statistics only** (refit per N to avoid leakage).\n- **Body style role:** both an input feature (one-hot) and a stratification axis (balanced draws + per-class metrics). Deliberate choice.\n- **No pre-existing splits/normalisation columns assumed** \u2014 we create them.\n\n## 3. Models & Hyperparameter policy\nFive architectures, ordered by expected data-hunger:\n\n| # | Model | Rationale (scalability tie) |\n|---|-------|------------------------------|\n| M1 | Linear + deg-2 Polynomial + Ridge (\u03bb via small inner CV) | Cheap floor; shows whether ML beats low-order structure at all. |\n| M2 | Gaussian Process, RBF + White kernel, standardized inputs | Small-data champion; O(N\u00b3) caps usable N; expected to lead at low N and lose at high N. |\n| M3 | Random Forest (n_estimators=400) | Nonparametric, no scaling, parallel; robust mid-N interpolator. |\n| M4 | Hist Gradient Boosting (n_estimators=300) | Strongest tree ensemble; better mid/large-N sample efficiency than RF. |\n| M5 | MLP 2\u00d7128 ReLU, batch-norm, early stopping | Large-data arch; expected to keep improving and overtake GP at high N \u2014 the crux of H2. |\n\n**HP policy \u2014 two variants:**\n- **V1 (HEADLINE): fixed, justified defaults** (values above). No per-model tuning \u2192 isolates the pure architecture\u00d7size effect; no tuning-budget confound.\n- **V2 (sensitivity check): tuned**, each model given an **equal, explicitly-capped inner-CV grid of \u226420 configs total** (5-fold inner CV, selected by inner R\u00b2). Explicit grids:\n  - M1: Ridge \u03bb \u2208 {1e-3, 1e-2, 0.1, 1, 10} (5).\n  - M2: length_scale \u2208 {0.5, 1, 2, 5, 10}, noise \u2208 {1e-4, 1e-3, 1e-2} (15).\n  - M3: n_estimators \u2208 {200, 400}, max_depth \u2208 {None, 12}, min_samples_leaf \u2208 {1, 2} (8).\n  - M4: learning_rate \u2208 {0.05, 0.1}, max_iter \u2208 {150, 300} (4).\n  - M5: hidden \u2208 {2\u00d764, 2\u00d7128, 2\u00d7256}, lr \u2208 {1e-3, 3e-4} (6).\n  All totals \u226420. **This is a fixed grid, NOT an Optuna/Bayesian sweep.**\n\n## 4. Size ladder & sampling protocol\n- **Ladder:** N \u2208 **{100, 250, 500, 1000, 2000, 5000, full}** (7 points; N=5000 added to resolve the mid\u2192high-N transition instead of inferring it).\n- **Test set:** one fixed **stratified-by-body-style** held-out set, **20%** of data, shared by all models and all N.\n- **Training draws:** remaining 80% = pool. For each N, draw a **nested, stratified, fixed-seed subset** so 100 \u2282 250 \u2282 \u2026 \u2282 5000 \u2282 full (monotone, no leakage; test never in training).\n- **Seeds:** **3 independent draw-seeds** per N to estimate draw-variance; report mean \u00b1 std across seeds.\n- **Leakage controls:** test disjoint from all training; standardization fit on training subset only; inner-CV folds never touch the test set.\n\n## 5. CV & Evaluation\n- **Outer (size-scaling evaluation):** the single fixed held-out test set; metrics computed once per (model, N, seed).\n- **Inner (model selection, V2 only):** 5-fold CV on the training subset at each N; folds disjoint from outer test.\n- **Metrics:** **RMSE (primary)**, **MAE**, **R\u00b2** \u2014 reported **both pooled** (all styles) and **per-body-style** (fastback / notchback / estateback).\n- **Primary result:** pooled RMSE. Per-class RMSE as secondary to localize architecture differences.\n\n## 6. Analysis plan (mapping to the two sub-questions)\n- **Sub-question 1 \u2014 scalability trend (H1):**\n  - *Figure 1:* learning curves \u2014 pooled RMSE vs N (log-x) for each of the 5 architectures, mean \u00b1 3-seed band. Monotone decrease per curve = H1 supported; slope = rate of improvement.\n  - *Table 1 (HEADLINE, V1 fixed-defaults):* pooled RMSE/MAE/R\u00b2 for each model \u00d7 each N.\n- **Sub-question 2 \u2014 architecture ranking (H2):**\n  - *Figure 2:* same learning curves but with **ranking annotated per N** (rank 1\u20135 at each N); a visible **crossover** (GP leads at low N, MLP overtakes at high N) is the key evidence.\n  - *Table 2:* per-body-style RMSE (model \u00d7 style \u00d7 N) to show whether a style drives the ranking.\n  - *Table 3 (SENSITIVITY, V2 tuned):* same as Table 1 but with tuned models; compared against Table 1 to show how much the ranking depends on tuning. **Not the primary result.**\n- **Statistical note:** with 3 seeds, report whether the low-N (GP) and high-N (MLP) gaps exceed the seed band; if borderline, note as \"suggestive, not conclusive\" and recommend more seeds.\n\n## 7. Feasibility & Compute budget\n- **Env prerequisite (stated plainly):** this design **assumes a pre-provisioned Python env** with `numpy`, `pandas`, `scikit-learn`, and `scikit-optimize` (or equivalent) / `GPyTorch` for the GP. **The current `/workspace` has none of these installed and network is disabled**, so anyone executing this must set up the environment (offline wheels / pre-built venv) **before** running. It is NOT currently available.\n- **Fits:** 7 N \u00d7 5 models \u00d7 3 seeds = **105 outer fits** (V1); V2 multiplies inner-CV selection by the \u226420-config grids per (model, N) but inner fits are cheap.\n- **GP wall-clock (heaviest):** at N=2000, O(N\u00b3) \u2248 8\u00d710\u2079 kernel-eval ops per fit; \u00d73 seeds \u00d71 kernel config (V1) \u2248 2.4\u00d710\u00b9\u2070 ops. On 224 CPU cores / multithreaded BLAS this is on the order of **seconds-to-a-few-minutes per fit** \u2014 comfortably tractable without a GPU. GP is capped at N\u22642000 (the 5000 and full points are excluded for the GP, or run with a sparse/inducing-point variant flagged as a variant).\n- **MLP:** \u226410k \u00d7 ~30 encoded features, 2\u00d7128, CPU \u2014 seconds per fit; trivial.\n- **Total:** realistically **< 1\u20132 wall-clock hours** including V2 tuning; well within budget.\n\n## 8. Limitations\n- **Unverified data specifics:** 26-param schema (23+3), per-class counts, total N, and Cd target are assumed from the paper, not inspected (dataset absent from repo). A schema mismatch (e.g., different categorical count) changes encoding and GP kernel design.\n- **GP coverage gap:** GP only run to N=2000 due to O(N\u00b3); its high-N behavior (5000, full) is not directly measured.\n- **Low seed count (3):** draw-variance bands may be too narrow to declare a crossover conclusive; flagged for possible increase.\n- **Single held-out test:** one 20% test set gives a point estimate per N; more repeated CV would tighten it at the cost of compute.\n- **No environment here:** cannot be executed in the current `/workspace` without first provisioning the Python environment (see \u00a77).\n- **Tuning confound:** V1 uses fixed defaults; V2 shows sensitivity but the two use different selection procedures, so V2 is a robustness check, not a fairer headline.", "evidence": "Folds researcher's 5 requests: (1) N=5000 added as 7th ladder point; (2) GP N=2000 one-line wall-clock: O(N^3)~8e9 ops/fit x3 seeds x1 config ~2.4e10 ops, seconds-to-minutes on 224 cores, no GPU; (3) V2 inner-CV grids explicit and each <=20 configs, stated as fixed grid not Optuna; (4) Table 1 (V1 fixed-defaults) is the headline primary result, Table 3 (V2 tuned) is a sensitivity check; (5) feasibility section plainly states a pre-provisioned env (numpy/pandas/scikit-learn/scikit-optimize) is required and current /workspace has none with network disabled. Structure follows requested 8 sections. Prior verified facts carried: /workspace has only task.json, no dataset, Python 3.14.4, no numpy/sklearn, 224 cores, no GPU.", "stage": "result", "summary": "Deliver full experiment design document"}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-bee42e5f90aa474ab182d2feb718f74b", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "critic_result": null, "summary": "Deliver full experiment design document", "kind": "ActionEvent"}
